Spliced Overpass
Ingredients: FUSION / JAZZ ROCK + CYBERPUNK & DYSTOPIAN + Snare
Creative Catalyst: It began as protest music performed on hacked traffic infrastructure.

Spliced Overpass is the sound of an iron bridge vibrating under toxic rain, where dizzying, mathematically complex polyrhythms collide with the grease and grit of urban decay. It pairs the frantic, high-register exploration of 1970s jazz-rock fusion with the cold, damp circuitry of a dystopian metropolis. The central anchor of the entire sonic architecture is the snare: heavily overdriven, gated to the point of brutality, yet capable of terrifying dynamic nuance. It snaps like an exposed live wire against wet concrete, laying down swing-heavy breakbeats that dissolve into free-form jazz freakouts before snapping back into lockstep mechanical precision.

Genre DNA
- Tempo & Pulse: 135–155 BPM; an uneasy, limping swing that feels like a train skipping slightly off its tracks.
- Harmonic Palette: Diminished-seventh clusters, modal jazz quartal voicings, and detuned microtonal intervals played on analog synths.
- Production Signatures: 1. Extreme parallel compression on the dry snare mic to capture every transient crack; 2. Sidechained tape-delay loops fed back into an analog filter bank; 3. Field recordings of pneumatic rail switches woven directly into the mix bus.
- Dynamics: Abrupt, violent drops where a blistering seven-minute modal jam instantly shears away into an isolated, dripping snare drum hit echoing across a concrete viaduct.

Scene & Origin Lore
In the rust-choked municipal arteries of Sector 4, outcasts originally hijacked the district's automated traffic-routing gantries to broadcast pirate jazz-rock jams using the infrastructure's piezoelectric buzzers as makeshift speakers. Out of this underground rebellion grew a tightly knit subculture of dockworkers, rogue coders, and disaffected horn players who wear heavy-duty waterproof coats lined with copper mesh and salvaged circuit boards. Their gatherings take place in subterranean parking levels beneath elevated expressways, where dancers move in erratic, angular bursts mimicking the erratic timing of faulty traffic signals.

Central Instruments: Unfretted electric bass with bridge-pickup bite, battered brass section (alto sax and muted trumpet), analog polyphonic synthesizer, and a heavily modified acoustic piccolo snare drum.

